The yield keyword is the difference between trying to drink from a firehose and sipping from a straw. 
While a standard return statement spits out an entire dataset all at once and terminates the function, 
yield pauses the function, handing you one piece of data at a time while remembering exactly where it left off.

Technical Breakdown :-
When Python sees yield inside a function, it automatically converts that function into a Generator.

The return way (Eager Evaluation): The function builds the entire result set in memory—like appending a million rows to a list—and then hands the massive list back.

The yield way (Lazy Evaluation): The function creates an iterator. When the pipeline asks for data (via a for loop or next()), the function wakes up, runs until it hits 
the yield statement, emits exactly one value, and instantly freezes its local variables in state. When asked again, it resumes perfectly from that frozen state to process the next item.

Buisness Context :- 
This is the ultimate defense against Out-of-Memory (OOM) crashes in Python data engineering. 
If you need to parse a 100-gigabyte JSON log file on a cheap Airflow worker node with only 4GB of RAM,
a standard function will attempt to load all 100GB into memory and instantly explode. By using yield,
you stream the file line-by-line, allowing the business to process petabytes of data on heavily constrained, inexpensive cloud hardware.

Design Decision :- 
The most common anti-pattern I see in code reviews is an engineer writing a beautiful, 
memory-efficient generator using yield to pull from a paginated REST API, only to have another developer downstream wrap that generator in a list()
call—instantly forcing the entire stream into RAM and defeating the entire purpose. Additionally, beyond data processing, I use yield extensively 
in Pytest fixtures for pipeline testing; it acts as the perfect context manager, cleanly setting up a mock database, 
yielding the connection to the test, and automatically tearing it down when the test finishes.

